WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:05.230
[music]

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:06.330
I'm Bruce Bower.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:09.880
I write about behavioral sciences for Science News.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.180
So I cover everything from psychology

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.400
to archaeology, anthropology...

00:00:14.409 --> 00:00:15.349
[laughs]

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.380
If it's about human beings and how we think and behave

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:20.260
then that's pretty much what my beat is.

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:21.940
Psychologists are currently going through

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:24.880
a period of much angst and worry

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:26.340
over whether their science

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:29.580
can really be found to repeat their findings --

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:30.720
whether it's valid,

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.460
are they really onto something?

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:33.740
There's much discussion now

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:37.260
about how psychology should pursue this issue

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:39.600
and how can it be solved.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.640
This is something that we are going to cover

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:44.400
quite a bit in Science News

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:46.380
because it applies not just psychology

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:48.620
but to many areas of science right now.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.079
For scientists, reproducibility is very important

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:52.900
because what means is that

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:57.020
when they get one particular finding it's important to

00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:59.480
know that people can find the same thing 

00:00:59.480 --> 00:01:01.160
in independent labs

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:03.000
with independent researchers

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.560
trying to see if the finding holds up. 

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:07.660
So one aspect of the way psychologists

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.520
currently do business with significance testing

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:15.920
is to focus purely on the average scores of groups

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:19.140
and to compare the averages of one group to another

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:21.980
and if one average is considered

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:24.820
to be statistically significant --

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:27.660
or different enough from the other group --

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:30.700
then they consider that to be an effect.

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:34.040
But averages can hide just as much as they reveal.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:38.000
And as we've recently reported in Science News,

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.580
it can be very instructive to look not at averages,

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:43.740
but at individual differences. 

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.320
So when researchers recently looked at

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.740
individual differences between young children

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:51.660
learning what they call theory of mind,

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:54.760
or how to understand and make inferences

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:56.760
about what others are thinking and feeling,

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:00.200
The children showed very different rates of learning --

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.920
even older couple of years, from age three to five.

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:06.120
And I think right now,

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:11.200
given a lot of the controversy and the anxiety

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:14.720
over significance testing and lack of reproducibility,

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:17.780
there may be more of a focus on individual differences,

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:20.080
which would be a very healthy move for the field.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:26.820
[music]

